# Copy to `.env.local` and adjust. `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is not.
#
# Nothing here is a real secret. The local database password matches
# docker-compose.yml and protects a throwaway container.

# The local PostgreSQL from docker-compose.yml. Port 55432, not 5432, to avoid
# colliding with an existing installation.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://sunsum:devpassword@localhost:55432/sunsum

# Which store the backend reads from.
#
#   mock  the in-memory BackendStore in core/store (the default)
#   db    the implemented PostgresBackendStore selected by composition.ts
#
# `npm run db:seed` populates the canonical local schema. With db selected, the
# workflow endpoints use DATABASE_URL and SUNSUM_DB_AUTH below. Database mode
# requires a reachable, migrated database and grants; it does not provision
# Azure or replace fixed demo identities with authenticated participants.
# Separate PG*/SUNSUM_DATABASE_AUTH operator tooling does not configure this store.
SUNSUM_STORE=mock

# How the backend proves who it is to PostgreSQL. Leave unset unless the
# hostname is misleading.
#
#   password  a password from DATABASE_URL (the local container)
#   entra     a Microsoft Entra access token (the Azure flexible server, which
#             is provisioned with password authentication disabled)
#
# Unset means "decide from the host": anything ending in
# .postgres.database.azure.com uses Entra, everything else uses the password.
# Set it explicitly when that inference is wrong — a private endpoint reached
# through an alias, or a developer pointing at the cloud server from a
# workstation with `az login`.
#
# SUNSUM_DB_AUTH=entra

# Which user-assigned managed identity to authenticate as, when the container
# has more than one. A system-assigned identity leaves this unset. Only read on
# the Entra path.
#
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=

# The key the session cookie is signed with. At least 32 characters.
#
# Sessions carry only a user id and the time they were issued; the role is read
# from the user row on every request, so a role that changes in the database
# takes effect immediately and a stolen cookie cannot claim a role it was not
# given. What the signature buys is that the user id cannot be edited.
#
# Unset in development, the backend generates one per process and says so. That
# means sessions end when the dev server restarts, which is a fair trade for
# needing no setup. In production an unset secret is refused outright rather
# than generated, because a per-instance secret presents as users being signed
# out at random and is miserable to diagnose.
#
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# (Require the module explicitly. The global `crypto` is the Web Crypto API and
# has no `randomBytes`; a bare `crypto` only works in `node -e` because of a
# legacy auto-require shim that does not apply in a real script.)
#
# SUNSUM_SESSION_SECRET=

# Whether POST /api/auth/demo-switch is reachable. Set it to `enabled` to turn
# it on; anything else, including unset, leaves it off.
#
# The demo sign-in hands out one of three *seeded* identities so the three
# roles can be shown without an identity provider. It is not a credential
# check: anyone who can reach the endpoint can become any of the three demo
# users, including the operator.
#
# It is therefore opt-in. A deployment that left this on by default while
# serving real records would be no better protected than one with no sign-in at
# all, so turning it on is a decision a deployment records rather than one it
# inherits. The demo deployment sets it; production leaves it unset once a real
# identity provider is wired up.
#
# SUNSUM_DEMO_AUTH=enabled
